### Blog API - test requests (VS Code REST Client)
### Install the "REST Client" extension, then click "Send Request" above any block.
### Order matters for the logged-in requests: register -> login -> me -> posts.

@base = http://localhost:3000
@postId = PASTE_A_POST_ID_HERE
@userId = PASTE_YOUR_USER_ID_HERE


# ============ AUTH ============

# Register. Creates a user and hashes the password with bcrypt (pre-save hook).
# Password must be at least 6 characters.
POST {{base}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "My Name",
  "email": "myname@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

###

# Register with a bad email to see the validation error (400)
POST {{base}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Bad Email",
  "email": "not-an-email",
  "password": "password123"
}

###

# Register the same email twice to see the unique index error (400)
POST {{base}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Duplicate",
  "email": "myname@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

###

# Login. Saves the session in Redis and sets a connect.sid cookie.
# The cookie is stored in cookies.txt automatically, so later requests stay logged in.
POST {{base}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "myname@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

###

# Login with a seeded user (password is password123 for all of them)
POST {{base}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "ada@seed.example",
  "password": "password123"
}

###

# Me. Reads the session back out of Redis. 401 if you are not logged in.
GET {{base}}/auth/me

###

# Logout. Destroys the session in Redis.
POST {{base}}/auth/logout


# ============ POSTS ============

# Get all posts. FIRST call returns "source":"db" (cache MISS) and fills the cache.
# Call it AGAIN within 60 seconds: "source":"cache" (cache HIT).
# Run `redis-cli GET posts:all` or `redis-cli TTL posts:all` to watch it happen.
GET {{base}}/posts

###

# Create a post. Requires login. Clears the cache and publishes a "new-post"
# event, which you should see printed in the server terminal.
POST {{base}}/posts
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "My First Post",
  "body": "Hello from the REST Client."
}

###

# Get one post. Replace PASTE_A_POST_ID_HERE with an id from the response above.
GET {{base}}/posts/{{postId}}

###

# Update a post. Requires login. Also clears the cache.
PUT {{base}}/posts/{{postId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "My First Post (edited)",
  "body": "This body was changed by an update request."
}

###

# Delete a post. Requires login. Also clears the cache.
DELETE {{base}}/posts/{{postId}}


# ============ STATS (aggregation) ============
# Seed first if you have no data: node scripts/seed.js

# Total number of users ($count)
GET {{base}}/stats/users-count

###

# Posts per user ($group, $lookup, $unwind, $project, $sort)
GET {{base}}/stats/posts-per-user

###

# Top 5 most liked posts + average and total likes ($sort, $limit, $group)
GET {{base}}/stats/popular

###

# Posts with at least 3 likes, page 1, 5 per page ($match, $sort, $skip, $limit)
GET {{base}}/stats/search?minLikes=3&page=1&limit=5

###

# Page 2 of the same search - notice the results are different posts
GET {{base}}/stats/search?minLikes=3&page=2&limit=5


# ============ HOW TO TEST THE 429 RATE LIMIT ============
#
# The limit is set in middleware/rateLimiter.js:
#   const LIMIT = 100;         <- max requests per IP
#   const WINDOW = 15 * 60;    <- 15 minutes, in seconds
#
# 1. To test quickly, open middleware/rateLimiter.js and change LIMIT to 5.
#    (Optionally change WINDOW to 60 for a 1 minute window.)
# 2. Restart the server: node server.js
# 3. Clear the old counter so you start from zero, otherwise the key already
#    exists and you will immediately be blocked:
#
#        redis-cli DEL "rate:::ffff:127.0.0.1"
#
#    (your key may differ, list them all with: redis-cli --scan --pattern "rate:*")
# 4. Send the request below more than 5 times in a row. The first 5 succeed,
#    the 6th and later return:
#        HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
#        {"error":"Too Many Requests"}
#
# 5. To reset again, delete the key (see step 3) or wait 15 minutes.
#    The window restarts automatically on the first request after the expiry.

GET {{base}}/

###

# The root route is the simplest one to spam, since it needs no login.
GET {{base}}/
